<h4>Project Description</h4>
<p>The goal of creating the expanded Human Oral Microbiome Database (eHOMD) is to provide the 
scientific community with comprehensive information on the microbes present in the human 
oral cavity and nose (the aerodigestive tract). The database contains genomic information 
on oral and nasal bacteria and the phages that infect them, and includes selected bacteria 
from other body sites as well as pathogens and environmental 
bacteria that can be detected in oral samples.

<p>Among 553 species of oral bacteria, approximately 52% are officially named, 
18% unnamed (but cultivated) and 29% are known only as uncultivated phylotypes. 
The HOMD presents a provisional naming scheme for the currently unnamed species 
so that strain, clone, and probe data from any laboratory can be directly linked 
to a stably named reference scheme. The HOMD links sequence data with phenotypic, 
phylogenetic, clinical, and bibliographic information. Genome sequences for oral 
bacteria determined as part of this project, the Human Microbiome Project, and other 
sequencing projects are being added to the HOMD as they become available.

<p>Genomes for 377 oral taxa (68% of oral taxa) are currently available on HOMD. 
The HOMD site offers easy-to-use tools for viewing and querying all publicly 
available oral bacterial genomes.
<br><br>This project is supported by grant R01 DE016937 and R56 DE016937 from 
The National Institute for Dental and Craniofacial Research.
